          "name": "Trithuria brevistyla  de Lange & Mosyakin",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "lineage": "Trithuria",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "A diminutive, tufted, aquatic herb 10–40 mm tall producing numerous unsheathed, fine hair-like leaves arranged in fans, with inconspicuous inflorescences borne on stalks 1–6 mm long and much-reduced, female-only flowers. Endemic to the South Island of New Zealand, it grows in the shallows of glacial lakes between 35 and 600 m a.s.l., often part of the aquatic-turf community. It differs from T. inconspicua in its shortened stigmatic hairs forming a knobbly capitate head. Classified Threatened – Nationally Vulnerable.",
